AWARDS AND PRESENTATIONS

2.. Mayor Carr to present proclamation for “National Nurses Day” to Fountain Valley Regional Hospital  -  Action: agenda title only {\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Mayor Carr introduced Marilou Salso, a nurse at Fountain Valley Hospital, in recognition of National Nurses Day, May 6.  Mayor Carr expressed appreciation for the dedication of all nurses regardless of where they work, especially this past year during Covid-19. \par
\par
Ms. Salso thanked Mayor Carr for the recognition and proclamation on behalf of all nurses at Fountain Valley Hospital.\par
}


3.. Mayor Carr to proclaim May as Asian American and Pacific Islander Heritage Month and present proclamation to Mary Adams Urashima for her passion for preserving Huntington Beach’s Asian history  -  Action: agenda title only {\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Mayor Carr thanked local resident and historian Mary Adams Urashima for her passion and diligence in preserving local Asian history in Huntington Beach.  Ms. Urashima, author of Historic Wintersburg, has been instrumental in documenting Asian American history in Orange County.\par
\par
Ms. Urashima expressed her appreciation to the Council for the recognition, and thanked other members of the community who have also served to ensure the preservation of this important history.  Ms. Urashima stated her pride at being involved in the process which resulted in Historic Wintersburg being designated a National Treasure and the creation of a PBS documentary.  \par
\par
Mayor Carr announced the creation of five vignettes that will be showing during May, as well as a Film Festival later in May presented by the Human Relations Task Force, to recognize local Asian American history.\par
}


4.. Mayor Carr to present the “Making a Difference Award” to Rachelle Crachiollo, Corinne Burton and Deanne Mendoza of Teacher Created Materials  -  Action: agenda title only {\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Mayor Carr thanked Jim Silva for nominating Teacher Created Materials for this award, and introduced Rachelle Crachiollo, Corinne Burton and Deanne Mendoza.  Ms. Crachiollo started Teacher Created Materials 40 years ago as a part-time venture which is now a thriving publishing business distributing product in 89 countries worldwide.  Teacher Created Materials is a leader in creating supplemental education resources which are engaging and easy to use.  Ms. Crachiollo's daughters Corinne and Deanne are both very instrumental in the success of Teacher Created Materials.  In addition to their successful business, all three women are also instrumental in the success of other organizations such as Read Across America, Ability First, Waymakers, the Cal State Fullerton US Scholarship Fund, National Disaster School Replenishment Club, Caterina's Club, and Breast Cancer Research.\par
\par
Councilmember Delgleize congratulated these women for being great role models and applauded them for the many ways they have helped teachers to be more successful.\par
\par
Ms. Mendoza expressed their gratitude for the recognition, and explained their top priorities are being responsible business owners and residents of Huntington Beach.  Ms. Mendoza acknowledged their passionate employees who work hard to create a world in which children love to learn.  Ms. Mendoza encouraged everyone to remember to thank a teacher during this Teacher Appreciation Week.\par
}


5.. Mayor Carr to proclaim May 2-8 as National Travel and Tourism Week and present proclamation to Visit HB CEO Kelly Miller  -  Action: agenda title only {\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Mayor Carr acknowledged National Travel &amp; Tourism Week and reviewed the impact of tourism in Huntington Beach, which in 2018 saw 3.74 million non-Orange County visitors generate over $565M in spending, supporting over 6,900 jobs.  Mayor Carr stated that Visit HB is doing an exceptional job of prioritizing local business recovery in a clean and safe environment, and supports diversity, equity and inclusion throughout the industry.\par
\par
Mr. Miller stated the Huntington Beach and Surf City USA brand is stronger than ever because of the challenges of the past year, and reiterated that Huntington Beach provides what people are looking for, i.e., open space, safety, walkability, and sunsets.  He thanked his team for their dedication to helping ensure that visitors have an incredible California experience in Huntington Beach.\par
}


